We had bad weather. You have fewer farmers. You have fewer acres. To make matters worse, chili grown in Mexico is often trucked over the border, labeled as New Mexico chili and sold for far cheaper than the farmers in the state can compete with. But Glenn says that there's one thing in particular that has him and other chili farmers especially worried. Chili farmers are used to bad weather, to competition, to all that other stuff. But now, even if they had perfect weather, perfect crop of chilies and no competition at all, they'd still have a
我们遇到了糟糕的天气。农户减少了。种植面积减少了。雪上加霜的是，在墨西哥种植的辣椒经常被卡车运过边境，贴上新墨西哥州辣椒的标签出售，价格比本州农户的成本低得多，根本无法竞争。但格伦说，有一件事尤其让他和其他辣椒农忧心忡忡。辣椒农习惯了恶劣天气、竞争以及所有其他风风雨雨。但现在，即便他们拥有完美的天气、完美的辣椒收成且没有任何竞争，他们依然会面临一个问题。

